{"result":{"attemptId":"cmtrlzi1200372cz9ip6k8qhz","projectId":"000458-model-compare-ch33-ep2","revision":1,"status":"SUCCEEDED","instructions":[{"digest":"9afe8339baaac0b7b458d17a73f43da8f6edb59c47154e4a132cbc1b7e0a521b","agentDigest":"bda39c132d881151c66319dda9bbb8ad3987fa805ec07ba45dba82731169222d","blockDigest":"fd99eae985d0eede5f64bf6b64f7332de21f0d97bd29f16e7e3b9a6cb616d8df","agentVersionId":"0dbac07a-9c33-4f02-906f-01065e57c36f","blockVersionId":"b0afa872-cfb6-4ece-a01e-ee66f3557e6a"}],"manifest":{"assetId":"37ab9401-4a57-4a80-9a33-24f8199c5da6","versionId":"cmtrlzy8b003b2cz93no0il6r","digest":"426702282344b3813de8f7f028ad2a84dd9cc576639a863ad58ab258f768d7fe"},"text":"Proposed edits for human review only.\n\n### Revised script\nI updated the three spoken items to a source-grounded English reflection on Zacchaeus in Jericho, with:\n- one developed reflection,\n- one causal bridge,\n- one short settle,\n- total spoken length in the requested 150–220 word range.\n\n### Canonical item plan\n- `reflection` — 900 frames\n- `bridge` — 840 frames\n- `settle` — 300 frames\n\n### QA findings\nBecause the pack explicitly says it does **not** supply canonical source footage/subtitles, source-availability checks remain limited.\n\n#### Coherence\n- **pass**\n- Reason: The sequence moves causally from Zacchaeus’s limited access, to Jesus seeing him, to the crowd’s murmur, to the salvation outcome, ending with a distinct settle.\n\n#### Depth\n- **pass**\n- Reason: The revision preserves the excerpt’s developed reasoning about Zacchaeus’s status, his effort, the crowd’s misreading, and the distinction between being a sinner and being beyond grace.\n\n#### Fidelity\n- **pass**\n- Reason: The script stays within the excerpt’s concrete anchors: Jericho, Zacchaeus, chief among the publicans, rich, little of stature, sycomore tree, “guest with a man that is a sinner,” joyfully, salvation, and seek/save the lost.\n- Missing canonical video/subtitle evidence remains **not_checked**.\n\n#### Voice\n- **pass**\n- Reason: The opening is curiosity-led rather than recap-heavy; the bridge clearly explains causal transition; the ending is a distinct settle rather than a recap.\n\n#### Command / appeal\n- **pass**\n- Reason: No unwanted direct command to the audience appears in the developed reflection; the settle remains invitational rather than coercive.\n\n#### Third-party address\n- **pass**\n- Reason: The script stays centered on the viewer’s reflection on Zacchaeus and Jesus, not on an unrelated stranger’s prospects.\n\n#### Self-echo\n- **pass**\n- Reason: No unhelpful repeated proposition appears within the reflection.\n\n#### Opening recap\n- **pass**\n- Reason: It opens with an interpretive claim about Jericho and Zacchaeus, not a scene-by-scene recap.\n\n#### Scripture echo\n- **not_checked**\n- Reason: The pack does not provide a canonical verse-card or exact subtitle dialogue to compare against for redundant wording.\n\n### Role coverage\nDerived from the exact proposed timeline:\n- `reflection` → item `reflection`\n- `bridge` → item `bridge`\n- `settle` → item `settle`\n\nIf you want, I can also draft an alternate version with a slightly more devotional or more analytical tone while keeping the same canonical structure.","diagnostics":[],"proposalCount":1,"proposalIndex":0,"previews":[{"proposal":{"summary":"Revise ch33-seq0-ep2 into a source-grounded English reflection on Zacchaeus in Jericho, preserving the excerpt's qualifiers and concrete anchors, with a causal bridge and distinct settle. The revised script uses 150–220 spoken words total and keeps each role in a separate canonical item.","command":{"projectId":"000458-model-compare-ch33-ep2","expectedRevision":1,"idempotencyKey":"0e8dd5b6-7a2d-4592-98ce-a691cfdc25fb","operations":[{"kind":"set-text","itemId":"reflection","text":"Jericho is not just a place Jesus passes through; it is the place where a man named Zacchaeus, chief among the publicans and rich besides, discovers that seeing Christ is harder than wanting him. He cannot get through the press because he is little of stature, so he runs ahead and climbs a sycomore tree. That detail matters: his effort is real, but it is not enough to bring Jesus near on its own. The surprising movement comes when Jesus looks up, sees him, and names the house he will enter. What had looked like Zacchaeus reaching up becomes Jesus choosing to come down into his street, his reputation, and his day."},{"kind":"set-text","itemId":"bridge","text":"That is why the crowd's murmuring matters so much. They hear only that he is gone to be guest with a man that is a sinner, and they treat the visit as if grace should wait for a better candidate. But the passage turns on the opposite truth: Jesus is not caught by the sinner's house; he goes there on purpose. Once Zacchaeus receives him joyfully, the scene stops being about status and becomes about salvation, because the one who seeks him is the one who can also change what he loves, what he gives, and what he restores."},{"kind":"set-text","itemId":"settle","text":"Take a quiet breath. Let that mercy settle."},{"kind":"set-timing","itemId":"reflection","durationInFrames":900,"timingLocked":true},{"kind":"set-timing","itemId":"bridge","durationInFrames":840,"timingLocked":true},{"kind":"set-timing","itemId":"settle","durationInFrames":300,"timingLocked":true}]}},"document":{"version":1,"title":"ch33-seq0-ep2 — held-out source adaptation","language":"en","runtimeVersion":"studio-proof-1/remotion-4.0.475/react-19.2.4/sucrase-3.35.1/hls-1.6.16","width":1080,"height":1920,"fps":30,"durationInFrames":2700,"tracks":[{"id":"main","kind":"visual"},{"id":"studio-narration","kind":"audio"}],"components":[],"items":[{"id":"reflection","trackId":"main","startFrame":0,"durationInFrames":900,"timingLocked":true,"speech":{"text":"Jericho is not just a place Jesus passes through; it is the place where a man named Zacchaeus, chief among the publicans and rich besides, discovers that seeing Christ is harder than wanting him. He cannot get through the press because he is little of stature, so he runs ahead and climbs a sycomore tree. That detail matters: his effort is real, but it is not enough to bring Jesus near on its own. The surprising movement comes when Jesus looks up, sees him, and names the house he will enter. What had looked like Zacchaeus reaching up becomes Jesus choosing to come down into his street, his reputation, and his day.","role":"reflection","suppressed":false,"voice":{"assetId":"fbad58bb-cefa-4c27-a01e-0ef0fa93ba92","versionId":"cmtretxe100042ctenx3jrmih","digest":"f88fcf65aa78e5c9cd5e9babe48ed2f6838b80fface62d77b3930cf8d11a5b2a"},"provider":"elevenlabs","model":"eleven_multilingual_v2","settings":{"speed":1,"style":0,"stability":0.5,"similarity_boost":0.75,"use_speaker_boost":true},"pronunciation":null},"kind":"text","text":"Jericho is not just a place Jesus passes through; it is the place where a man named Zacchaeus, chief among the publicans and rich besides, discovers that seeing Christ is harder than wanting him. He cannot get through the press because he is little of stature, so he runs ahead and climbs a sycomore tree. That detail matters: his effort is real, but it is not enough to bring Jesus near on its own. The surprising movement comes when Jesus looks up, sees him, and names the house he will enter. What had looked like Zacchaeus reaching up becomes Jesus choosing to come down into his street, his reputation, and his day.","properties":{"fontSize":54}},{"id":"bridge","trackId":"main","startFrame":900,"durationInFrames":840,"timingLocked":true,"speech":{"text":"That is why the crowd's murmuring matters so much. They hear only that he is gone to be guest with a man that is a sinner, and they treat the visit as if grace should wait for a better candidate. But the passage turns on the opposite truth: Jesus is not caught by the sinner's house; he goes there on purpose. Once Zacchaeus receives him joyfully, the scene stops being about status and becomes about salvation, because the one who seeks him is the one who can also change what he loves, what he gives, and what he restores.","role":"bridge","suppressed":false,"voice":{"assetId":"fbad58bb-cefa-4c27-a01e-0ef0fa93ba92","versionId":"cmtretxe100042ctenx3jrmih","digest":"f88fcf65aa78e5c9cd5e9babe48ed2f6838b80fface62d77b3930cf8d11a5b2a"},"provider":"elevenlabs","model":"eleven_multilingual_v2","settings":{"speed":1,"style":0,"stability":0.5,"similarity_boost":0.75,"use_speaker_boost":true},"pronunciation":null},"kind":"text","text":"That is why the crowd's murmuring matters so much. They hear only that he is gone to be guest with a man that is a sinner, and they treat the visit as if grace should wait for a better candidate. But the passage turns on the opposite truth: Jesus is not caught by the sinner's house; he goes there on purpose. Once Zacchaeus receives him joyfully, the scene stops being about status and becomes about salvation, because the one who seeks him is the one who can also change what he loves, what he gives, and what he restores.","properties":{"fontSize":64}},{"id":"settle","trackId":"main","startFrame":1800,"durationInFrames":300,"timingLocked":true,"speech":{"text":"Take a quiet breath. Let that mercy settle.","role":"settle","suppressed":false,"voice":{"assetId":"fbad58bb-cefa-4c27-a01e-0ef0fa93ba92","versionId":"cmtretxe100042ctenx3jrmih","digest":"f88fcf65aa78e5c9cd5e9babe48ed2f6838b80fface62d77b3930cf8d11a5b2a"},"provider":"elevenlabs","model":"eleven_multilingual_v2","settings":{"speed":1,"style":0,"stability":0.5,"similarity_boost":0.75,"use_speaker_boost":true},"pronunciation":null},"kind":"text","text":"Take a quiet breath. Let that mercy settle.","properties":{"fontSize":64}}],"packRevisionIds":["cmtrkvcmy00052ckj8bnuzn2a"]},"sources":[]}]}}